      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fairmont was founded in 1819 as Middletown, Virginia, before becoming the county seat of Marion County in 1842.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairmont%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Boaz Fleming, a Revolutionary War veteran from Delaware, named the town Middletown after his home state and later renamed it Fairmont in 1820.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fairmontwv.gov/440/Fairmonts-Founding-History",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Baltimore and Ohio Railroad ran through Fairmont, making it a key stop on the line from Cumberland to Wheeling.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairmont%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fairmont Normal School, established as a teachers’ college, moved to its current campus in 1917 and later became Fairmont State University.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
